==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|strike|er}}

===Pronunciation===
{{rfp|en}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-striker.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪkə(ɹ)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An individual who is [[on strike]].
#: {{synonyms|en|turnout}}
# Someone or something that hits someone or something else.
## A [[blacksmith]]'s assistant who wields the [[sledgehammer]].
## A piece used to push other pieces toward the pockets in the Asian game of [[carom]].
# {{lb|en|soccer}} One of the players on a team in [[football]] ([[soccer]]) in the row nearest to the opposing team's [[goal]], who are therefore principally responsible for [[scoring]] goals.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 28, 2011|author=Tom Rostance|title=Arsenal 2 - 1 Olympiakos| work=BBC Sport| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14998317.stm| passage=Olympiakos had barely been in the Arsenal half but should have levelled in the 14th minute. A low corner was not dealt with and the ball fell to the feet of '''striker''' Rafik Djebbour, who saw his close-range effort brilliantly cleared from the goalline by Arteta.}}
# {{lb|en|military|slang}} An [[officer]]'s servant or [[orderly]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The English of Business|author=Franklyn Bliss Snyder & ‎Ronald Salmon Crane| year=1921| page=90| passage="Dog-robber" has a definite significance to some army men; but unless one has spent some time in uniform he will probably have to search long for its meaning: an officer's servant or '''striker.'''}}
# {{lb|en|baseball|slang|1800s}} The [[batter]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} The [[batsman]] who is currently facing the [[bowler]] and defending his [[wicket]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[harpoon]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[harpooner]].
#* {{rfdatek|en|Dampier}}
#*: Wherever we come to an anchor, we always send out our '''strikers''', and put out hooks and lines overboard, to try fish.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An inexperienced member of a ship's crew.
#: {{synonyms|en|green hand}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[wencher]]; a [[lewd]] [[man]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1621–6|year_published=1845| chapter={{w|The Unnatural Combat}}| title=The Plays of Philip Massinger| author={{w|Philip Massinger}}| editor=William Gifford| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6WtVAAAAcAAJ| others=Act 4, Scene 2| page=54| passage='Twill prove a notable '''striker''', like his father.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|politics}} A [[blackmailer]] in [[politics]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|politics}} One whose [[political]] [[influence]] can be [[buy|bought]].

{{Webster 1913}}

